[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a Research Square logo/wordmark. It contains a stylized geometric icon at left and the text “Research Square” in blue and green. No scientific panels, data plots, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, labels, or findings are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 1: Landscape of TeamPath (a) Steps of dataset curation. We extract image-text pairs from a processed TCGA dataset (PathGen-1.6M). (b) Word cloud visualization of ROI captions (upper) and questions (bottom). (c) The core visual language model architecture of TeamPath. (d) TeamPath as a system with an LLM-enhanced router (with over 80% accuracy in choosing the correct approach) and the corresponding capacities in various downstream applications. The logo fire means that we need to adjust the parameters of models, and the logo snowflake means that we do not change the parameters. (e) Overall ranking list of different methods across tasks and metrics. A lower rank (larger bubble) means a better method.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about pathology vision-language model dataset curation, training, and evaluation.\n\nPanel **a**: Workflow schematic starting from a **whole-slide histopathology image** and zoomed **regions of interest**. It shows data retrieval and agent summary steps, then PathGen-1.6M / PathInstruct-Reason style instruction data generation, including QA pairs, transcriptomic pairs, and QA pairs with reasoning. A dataset statistics box lists HEST-1K/STImage1K4M multi-omic datasets and measured genes.\n\nPanel **c**: VLM training architecture diagram. It includes an **Expert Vis Encoder**, **Expert Lang Encoder**, and **Expert Lang Decoder**, with pathology image patches/tokens and a text prompt leading to an answer output.\n\nPanel **d**: TeamPath router-based multi-task system. A task-specific instruction and pathology image region are sent to an SFT-trained router, which routes to Expert A, Expert B, or Expert C with RL/SFT/TTS indicators. The right box lists downstream tasks: spatial transcriptomic generation, reasoning-driven VQA, reasoning-driven summary, and AI-physician collaboration.\n\nPanel **e**: Bubble plot comparing models across pathology VQA, pathology summary, and generation metrics. Rows include TeamPath-7B, PathGen-LLaVA-13B, Patho-R1-7B, Qwen2.5VL-7B, MedGemma-4B, InternVL3-8B, Qwen2.5VL-3B, and MedVLThinker-7B. Columns include PubMed, SocialPath, Atlas, EduContent, PathCLS, BLEU, ROUGE-1/2/L, BERT, MEDCON, SPCC, GPCC, MSE, and AvgRank. Bubble size encodes rank from 1 to 8, with TeamPath-7B visually strongest across many metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 2: Benchmarking results with PathMMU for the pathology VQA task. We note that since we did not have information about the testing setting of PathGene-LLaVA-13B, we used results reported by the model creators in (23). (a) Accuracy across different categories of all selected methods with all samples. (b) Accuracy across different categories of all selected methods with samples from a tiny set. (c) Accuracy across different categories of all selected methods with samples from a large set. (d) Joint visualization with accuracy and ranking information for all selected methods. The darker the bubble color, the higher the model score; The larger the bubble shape, the lower the model ranking.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific performance figure with panels labeled **a**, **b**, **c**, and **d**.\n\nPanel **a**: Bar chart titled **“Type: overall”**. It compares multiple models across categories **PubMed**, **SocialPath**, **Atlas**, **EduContent**, **PathCLS**, and **Avg**. The legend lists models including **TeamPath-7B**, **Patho-R1-7B**, **Qwen2.5VL-3B**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B**, **InternVL3-8B**, **MedGemma-4B**, **MedVLMlinker-7B**, **PathGen-LLaVA-13B**, and **Random**. The y-axis shows scores roughly from 20 to 80+.\n\nPanel **b**: Bar chart titled **“Type: tiny_test”**, using the same model legend and task categories. It shows model scores for the tiny test subset.\n\nPanel **c**: Bar chart titled **“Type: large_test”**, again comparing the same models across the same task categories.\n\nPanel **d**: Bubble plot comparing models by task category. Rows are models, columns are **PubMed**, **SocialPath**, **Atlas**, **EduContent**, **PathCLS**, and **Avg**. Bubble size represents rank, with a legend for **Rank 1**, **Rank 3**, **Rank 5**, and **Rank 9**. Bubble color represents score, with a vertical color bar labeled **Score**.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, tissue samples, cells, or anatomical structures are visible. The figure presents quantitative benchmarking results for vision-language or medical/pathology-related models. The visible finding is that **TeamPath-7B** and **Patho-R1-7B** generally achieve the highest scores across most datasets and splits, while **Random** is lowest."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 3: Case study (topic: synaptophysin, which is a precursor cell that develops into an adipocyte (fat cell)) based on the outputs from different models. We highlight the correct information with green text and incorrect information with red text. For the models with errors, we consider two cases. The first case is a wrong answer, and the second case is a confused reasoning path.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a composite scientific/biomedical AI evaluation figure. It contains a small histopathology microscopy crop in the upper left showing adipose-like tissue with vacuolated cells and arrow markers. The central question asks which histologic feature is indicative of lipoblasts, with multiple-choice options A-D and an annotated answer stating option B.\n\nThe rest of the figure shows labeled response panels from several vision-language or pathology models: `Patho-R1`, `MedGemma-4B`, `Qwen2.5VL-7B`, `MedVLThinker-7B`, `InternVL3-8B`, `PathGen-LLaVA`, and `TeamPath-7B`. Text coloring indicates correctness: green text marks correct information and red text marks wrong information. Several panels propose option C, while the bottom `TeamPath-7B` panel supports option B.\n\nThe figure compares model reasoning on a pathology image question about lipoblast morphology, especially fat vacuoles causing nuclear indentation versus large clear vacuoles displacing the nucleus. The visual finding emphasized by the figure is that `TeamPath-7B` aligns with the annotated answer B, while multiple other models give answer C."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 10, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\nPanel **a** shows schematic workflows involving histopathology image inputs and question/answer text boxes. The top workflow includes a **Verifier** module, an **Answer Corrector** module, a feedback loop, rejection/check icons, and a final **Corrected Answer** output. The lower workflow shows a **Reason Corrector** receiving a question, wrong reason, and proposed answer, then outputting **Correct Reason** and **Corrected Answer**. Small microscopy thumbnails and clinician/avatar icons are shown at the left.\n\nPanel **b** is a bar chart comparing **Expert** and **Corrector** performance across datasets labeled **PubMed**, **SocialPath**, **Atlas**, **EduContent**, **PathCLS**, and **Avg**. Green bars represent Expert and pale pink bars represent Corrector. The plot reports **P-value = 0.0004**, with Corrector bars generally higher than Expert bars.\n\nPanel **c** shows a large H&E-like histopathology microscopy image with dense purple/pink stained cells. Adjacent text boxes show a visual question-answering example about characteristic features of nuclei in the image. The question asks what features are observed in cell nuclei. Answer options include hyperchromatic round nuclei, small elongated nuclei, intranuclear inclusions, and large vesicular nuclei with prominent nucleoli. Color-coded annotations indicate green text as correct information and red text as wrong information. The example contrasts an expert answer/reason with a “TeamPath answer/reason,” discussing nuclear size, chromatin appearance, nucleoli prominence, and why answer A is selected instead of D."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 12, "caption": "Figure 5: Benchmarking results of the caption summary task. (a) Performances of different methods for summarizing the caption based on ROI-level information across all metrics. We report the average scores and scaled standard deviation (0.1*sd) with all samples in the testing set. (b) Joint visualization with metric scores and ranking information for all selected methods. The darker the bubble color, the higher the model score; The larger the bubble shape, the lower the model ranking. (c) ROUGE-L and BERT scores based on samples from the selected disease across all methods. (d) ROUGE-L and BERT scores based on samples from the selected tissue across all methods. (e) A case study of caption summary generation based on TeamPath.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-e** comparing medical/pathology vision-language models on histopathology report/summary generation metrics.\n\nPanel **a**: Grouped bar chart of model scores across metrics **BLEU, ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, ROUGE-L, BERT, MEDCON**. Models listed in the legend include **TeamPath-7B, PathGen-LLaVA-13B, MedGemma-4B, Qwen2.5VL-3B, Qwen2.5VL-7B, Patho-R1-7B, InternVL3-8B, MedVLThinker-7B**. Bars have small error bars. BERT scores are highest overall, while BLEU is low across models.\n\nPanel **b**: Bubble/rank visualization with rows for the same models and columns **BLEU, ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, ROUGE-L, BERT, MEDCON, Average**. Bubble size encodes rank, with a legend for **Rank 1, Rank 3, Rank 5**; color encodes score using a vertical blue scale bar labeled **Score**. TeamPath-7B appears strongest overall, with large/high-scoring bubbles in several columns.\n\nPanels **c** and **d**: Subgroup grouped bar charts for cancer types:\n- **c**: Score for **Adenocarcinoma**, comparing **ROUGE-L** and **BERT**.\n- **d**: Score for **Breast**, comparing **ROUGE-L** and **BERT**.\nBoth panels use the same model color scheme as panel a. BERT scores are consistently high, while ROUGE-L varies more strongly across models.\n\nPanel **e**: Example model interface/output comparison. Left side shows the prompt: “Provide a concise pathological summary of the tissue shown in this histopathology image, highlighting any abnormal cellular or structural features in one paragraph.” Center shows a histopathology microscopy image with pink/red stained tissue. Right side compares generated text boxes labeled **Record** and **TeamPath**. Yellow highlighting marks “concept matched context.” The visible biological/pathological concepts include **elongated spindle-shaped cells**, **eosinophilic cytoplasm**, **elongated nuclei**, **smooth muscle cells**, **disorganized arrangement**, and **extracellular matrix**. The TeamPath output additionally mentions **moderate pink-staining extracellular matrix** and **connective tissue presence**.\n\nOverall finding shown visually: the figure benchmarks multiple pathology-oriented VLMs, with **TeamPath-7B** generally ranking highest across text similarity and medical concept metrics, and includes a qualitative histopathology summary example with concept-level text matching."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 14, "caption": "Figure 6: Evaluation of model performances for transcriptomic profile generation. (a) SPCC (higher is better) and MSE (lower is better) scores across different methods for the brain tissue. We report the average scores and scaled standard deviation (0.1*sd) for better visualization. (b) SPCC and MSE scores across different methods for the IDC samples. We report the average scores and scaled standard deviation (0.1*sd) for better visualization. (c) UMAP visualization from the testing set of brain to compare the generated results between Patho-R1 (base) and TeamPath colored by data sources. (d) UMAP visualization from the testing set of IDC to compare the generated results between Patho-R1 (base) and TeamPath colored by data sources. (e) Comparison of expression profiles between generated data and real data based on the brain tissue. (f) Comparison of expression profiles between generated data and real data based on the IDC samples.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-f**, comparing generated and reference biological data across methods.\n\nVisible content:\n- **Panels a and b:** Bar plots comparing models/methods including **TeamPath-7B**, **PathGen-LLAVA-13B**, **MedGemma-4B**, **Qwen2.5-VL-3B**, **Qwen2.5-VL-7B**, **Patho-R1-7B**, **InternVL3-8B**, **MedVLMThinker-7B**, and **Cell2Sentence-1B**.\n  - Metrics shown: **SPCC** and **MSE**.\n  - Panel **a** appears to show one dataset/task where TeamPath has the highest SPCC and lowest MSE.\n  - Panel **b** shows another task/dataset where TeamPath again has much higher SPCC and lower MSE than other methods.\n\n- **Panels c and d:** UMAP scatter plots comparing **Generated** versus **Reference** distributions.\n  - Legend: blue = **Generated**, orange = **Reference**.\n  - Panel **c** is labeled **Brain** and compares **Patho-R1** with **TeamPath**.\n  - Panel **d** is labeled **IDC** and compares **Patho-R1** with **TeamPath**.\n  - Patho-R1 generated points form visually separated or abnormal structures relative to reference points, while TeamPath generated points overlap more with reference distributions.\n\n- **Panels e and f:** Heatmaps comparing **TeamPath** outputs with **Real data**.\n  - Panel **e** shows paired heatmaps titled **TeamPath** and **Real data**, with categorical color bars along the bottom labeled from approximately **0 to 12**.\n  - Panel **f** shows another pair of heatmaps, also comparing TeamPath and real data, with bottom categories labeled approximately **0 to 17**.\n  - Heatmaps use blue-white-red intensity scales with numeric color bars at the right.\n\nBiological source objects/data:\n- Single-cell or spatial/transcriptomic-style biological data visualized as UMAP embeddings and expression-like heatmaps.\n- Named biological contexts visible: **Brain** and **IDC**.\n\nModel/interface context:\n- The figure compares multiple vision-language or biomedical generative models against **TeamPath**.\n- Outputs are evaluated against reference/real data using correlation/error metrics, embedding overlap, and heatmap similarity.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- **TeamPath** appears to best match reference/real biological data, with superior SPCC/MSE metrics and closer generated-reference alignment in UMAP and heatmap visualizations."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 29, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 1: A flowchart of data-preprocessing used to train TeamPath.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow-style scientific figure with two labeled panels, `a` and `b`.\n\nPanel `a` shows a pipeline for histopathology image processing and reasoning tasks:\n- “Download whole-slide histopathology images from TCGA: 9,568”\n- “Match the images with annotation from PathGen 1.6M: 5,650”\n- “Process the dataset to extract ROI: 1,620,876”\n- “Match the ROI with paired question-answer sets: 200,899”\n\nText annotations beside the lower steps indicate downstream uses:\n- “Reasoning-driven Summary”\n- “Reasoning-driven VQA AI-Physician Collaboration”\n\nPanel `b` shows two spatial transcriptomic generation pipelines:\n- Top branch: “Extract ROI with 224 radius from the IDC dataset: 44,974” followed by “Pair radius with transcriptomic data after filtering: 35,536”\n- Bottom branch: “Extract ROI with 224 radius from the Brain dataset: 47,443” followed by “Pair ROI with transcriptomic data after filtering: 47,443”\n\nBiological source objects visible in the labels include whole-slide histopathology images from TCGA, regions of interest (ROI), IDC dataset material, Brain dataset material, and transcriptomic data. The figure describes dataset filtering, ROI extraction, annotation/question-answer matching, and pairing image regions with transcriptomic data. No quantitative findings beyond dataset counts are shown."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 30, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a composite evaluation figure showing a medical visual question-answering task on an immunohistochemical pathology image.\n\nVisible content:\n- Top-left: a small histopathology/IHC image patch with pink tissue morphology and brown staining.\n- Header text: “Green means right info. Red means wrong info.”\n- Question: asks which marker is most likely targeted based on the staining pattern and color in “section A.”\n- Options: Estrogen Receptor, S100 protein, Synaptophysin, CD34.\n- Annotated answer: “(C) Synaptophysin.”\n\nModel/interface panels:\n- Multiple rounded text boxes represent outputs from different models:\n  - Patho-R1\n  - MedGemma-4B\n  - Qwen2.5VL-7B\n  - MedVLThinker-7B\n  - InternVL3-8B\n  - PathGen-LLaVA\n  - TeamPath-7B\n- Red text marks incorrect reasoning or wrong answers.\n- Green text marks correct reasoning or correct information.\n- Several panels include thumbs-down icons or red X markers indicating incorrect responses.\n- The TeamPath-7B panel at the bottom has a thumbs-up icon and green-highlighted correct conclusion.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The biological image source is a stained tissue section from immunohistochemistry.\n- The visible staining is described in the figure text as brown/cytoplasmic or strong brown staining.\n- The task concerns identifying an immunohistochemical marker from stain pattern and color.\n\nFindings shown:\n- The reference answer is Synaptophysin.\n- Several models incorrectly select Estrogen Receptor or S100 protein.\n- TeamPath-7B is presented as giving the correct answer, concluding that the staining corresponds to option C."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 31, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 3: Training strategies optimization with different settings. The results are evaluated based on the PathMMU dataset. (a) Accuracy across different categories based on the base model and different RL strategies. (b) Accuracy and rank across different categories based on the base model and different RL strategies. (c) Accuracy across different categories based on different base models and different RL/SFT strategies. (d) Accuracy and rank across different categories based on different base models and different RL/SFT strategies.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel quantitative scientific figure with four labeled panels: **a**, **b**, **c**, and **d**.\n\nPanel **a** shows a grouped bar chart comparing three model variants across datasets/tasks labeled **PubMed**, **SocialPath**, **Atlas**, **EduContent**, **PathCLS**, and **Avg**. The legend includes **Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO)**, **Patho-R1-7B**, and **Patho-R1-7B+RL (DAPO)**. Bars indicate performance scores, with the y-axis ranging roughly from 40 to 80.\n\nPanel **b** shows a bubble/scatter comparison for the same three Patho-R1 model variants across the same task labels. Bubble color and size encode performance, with a vertical color scale at right.\n\nPanel **c** shows a grouped bar chart comparing multiple models, including **Qwen2.5VL-7B+SFT**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B+SFT+RL (GRPO)**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B+RL**, **Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO)**, and **Patho-R1-7B** across the same task categories. Scores span approximately 20 to 80.\n\nPanel **d** shows the corresponding bubble/scatter comparison for the models in panel c, again using task labels on the x-axis and model names on the y-axis with a color scale at right.\n\nNo biological source objects such as microscopy images, cells, tissues, organs, or molecular structures are directly visible. The figure appears to evaluate biomedical/pathology-oriented vision-language or reasoning models on benchmark categories including PubMed, SocialPath, Atlas, EduContent, and PathCLS. The visible finding is that **Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO)** appears among the strongest-performing models overall, especially in the average column, while Qwen-based baselines generally score lower."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 32, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel grouped bar chart titled “Comparisons between data used for RL training.” The x-axis categories are PubMed, SocialPath, Atlas, EduContent, PathCLS, and Avg. The y-axis ranges roughly from 40 to 80.\n\nTwo conditions are compared:\n- Light green bars: “mc VQA”\n- Light blue bars: “full VQA”\n\nVisible findings:\n- PubMed: mc VQA slightly higher than full VQA.\n- SocialPath: full VQA slightly higher than mc VQA.\n- Atlas: both are highest overall, with full VQA slightly higher.\n- EduContent: mc VQA slightly higher than full VQA.\n- PathCLS: mc VQA much higher than full VQA.\n- Avg: mc VQA higher than full VQA.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, molecular diagrams, or model-interface screenshots are visible. This is a quantitative comparison plot of performance across datasets or evaluation categories."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 33, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 5: An example of using TeamPath to correct the reasoning path from pathologists.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a composite scientific/biomedical figure with multiple panels:\n\n- Left panel: A histology microscopy image, likely H&E-stained tissue. It shows epithelial cells arranged around small tubular or gland-like lumens, with pink stromal/background tissue and purple nuclei. The visible biological source object is a stained tissue section.\n- Middle-left panel: A question prompt about identifying a true statement regarding cell lining structure, with options A-D. Option D is highlighted in green: “The cells have cuticular borders suggesting a level of specialization.”\n- Middle panels: An “Expert answer” interface marked in red, stating “I do not know,” followed by an expert reasoning box explaining uncertainty and discussing brush borders, squamous epithelium, goblet cells, and cuticular borders.\n- Right panel: A “TeamPath answer” interface marked in green, selecting option D, with reasoning that the tubular structures resemble renal proximal tubules and that smooth homogeneous cuticular border lining suggests specialized/transitional epithelium. It also explains why goblet cells, intestinal epithelium, and squamous epithelium are excluded.\n- Labels/color coding: Text at the top states “Green means right info” and “Red means wrong info.” The expert response is red-coded as incorrect/unknown, while the TeamPath response is green-coded as correct.\n\nThe figure presents a comparison between an expert/model answer and a TeamPath answer for a histology multiple-choice question, highlighting that TeamPath selected the intended correct option based on visible epithelial/tubular morphology."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 34, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel grouped bar chart comparing model or method performance across datasets/categories.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis labels: `PubMed`, `SocialPath`, `EduContent`, `PathCLS`, `Atlas`, `Avg`\n- Y-axis range: 0 to 1, with horizontal gridlines at roughly 0.1 intervals\n- Legend entries:\n  - `Raw` in light blue\n  - `Corrector` in light green\n  - `o3` in gray\n  - `o4-mini` in beige\n\nApproximate findings:\n- `Raw` and `Corrector` are similar across most categories.\n- `o3` generally performs higher than `Raw`/`Corrector`, especially on `SocialPath`, `EduContent`, and `Atlas`.\n- `o4-mini` is highest on `PubMed`, `PathCLS`, `EduContent`, and average, reaching about 1.0 on `EduContent`.\n- Average performance appears highest for `o4-mini`, followed by `o3`, then `Raw`/`Corrector`.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, molecular diagrams, or experimental transformations are visible. This figure is a quantitative evaluation plot rather than a biological image."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 35, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 7: Examples of model outputs for caption summary tasks. We highlight both correct and incorrect information.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a comparative scientific figure showing model-generated pathology interpretations across multiple labeled panels. It appears to evaluate visual-language model outputs against a histopathology image prompt, using color highlighting.\n\nVisible panels/models:\n- TeamPath\n- MedGemma-4B\n- Qwen2.5VL-7B\n- InternVL3-8B\n- PathGen-LLaVA-13B\n- Qwen2.5VL-3B\n- Patho-R1-7B\n- MedVLthinker-7B\n\nVisual encoding:\n- Yellow highlight indicates concept-matched context.\n- Red highlight indicates incorrect context.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Tissue image descriptions refer to histopathology/microscopy of tissue sections.\n- Mentioned biological/pathology features include spindle-shaped cells, smooth muscle cells, collagenized stroma, necrosis, inflammatory or reparative processes, atypical cells, mitotic activity, cystic spaces, necrotic cardiac muscle fibers, neutrophilic debris, and mucin.\n\nModel interface/content:\n- Each panel contains a natural-language diagnostic caption generated by a different model.\n- The figure compares whether generated descriptions match relevant pathological concepts or contain incorrect findings.\n\nFindings shown:\n- TeamPath includes yellow-highlighted matched terms such as “smooth muscle cells,” “well organized pattern,” and “extracellular matrix.”\n- Several other models contain extensive red-highlighted incorrect pathology interpretations, including claims of inflammatory/neoplastic processes, necrosis, proliferative processes, malignancy, or atypical cells.\n- MedGemma-4B, InternVL3-8B, and some others contain both yellow matched context and red incorrect context.\n- The figure is intended to illustrate variation and errors in model-generated histopathology descriptions."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 36, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 8: Extended analyses of spatial transcriptomic generation. (a) GPCC across different methods based on the brain tissue. (b) GPCC across different methods based on the IDC sample. (c) Comparison between finetuned Qwen2.5VL-7B and TeamPath based on the brain tissue. (d) Comparison between finetuned Qwen2.5VL-7B and TeamPath based on the IDC sample.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel bar-chart figure with panels labeled **a**, **b**, **c**, and **d**.\n\nPanel **a**: Bar chart of **GPCC** across several model labels on the x-axis, including TeamPath-7B, PathGen-LLaVA-13B, MedGemma-4B, Qwen2.5VL-3B, Qwen2.5VL-7B, Patho-R1-7B, InternVL2-38B, MedVInThinker-7B, and Cell2Sentence-1B. TeamPath-7B has the highest positive GPCC; Qwen2.5VL-7B is negative.\n\nPanel **b**: Bar chart of **GPCC** for the same/similar model set. TeamPath-7B is much higher than all other models, with Qwen2.5VL-3B showing a smaller positive value.\n\nPanel **c**: Three side-by-side pairwise comparisons between **TeamPath-7B** and **Qwen2.5VL-7B(ft)** for **SPCC**, **MSE**, and **GPCC**. TeamPath-7B has higher SPCC, lower MSE, and slightly lower GPCC than Qwen2.5VL-7B(ft).\n\nPanel **d**: Three side-by-side pairwise comparisons between **TeamPath-7B** and **Qwen2.5VL-7B(ft)** for **SPCC**, **MSE**, and **GPCC**. TeamPath-7B has slightly higher SPCC, similar MSE, and substantially higher GPCC.\n\nVisible content is quantitative model-performance evaluation with error bars. No biological source objects, microscopy images, tissue samples, molecular structures, or model-interface screenshots are visible in this extracted figure."]}, {"figure_index": 16, "page_no": 37, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is an annotated biomedical visual question-answering panel. It contains a small histology microscopy crop at the upper left showing pink/purple H&E-stained tissue with large clear adipocyte-like spaces and epithelial/stromal tissue.\n\nVisible text elements include:\n\n- A color key: “Green means right info. Red means wrong info.”\n- A question asking: “What type of epithelial lining is present in the cystic structure visible in the image?”\n- Multiple-choice options:\n  - A: Stratified squamous epithelium\n  - B: Simple ciliated columnar epithelium\n  - C: Simple cuboidal epithelium\n  - D: Transitional epithelium\n- Annotated answer: “B Simple ciliated columnar epithelium.”\n- A model-generated reasoning box labeled “Think,” with green and red color-coded statements marking correct and incorrect reasoning.\n- A model/interface label at bottom right: “TeamPath-7B.”\n\nThe figure appears to evaluate a pathology VQA model response. The biological source object is a histological tissue image associated with a cystic epithelial structure, discussed in the text as serous cystadenoma/ovarian cystadenoma. The main finding presented is that the annotated answer is option B, simple ciliated columnar epithelium, while parts of the model explanation are marked as correct or incorrect using green/red text."]}]

